#!/usr/bin/ucode
// The devlan zone: the devices this gateway serves on its LAN ports.
//
//   pulzr-lan apply                          counting and forwarding table,
//                                              and each client's policy rules
//   pulzr-lan status                         JSON: clients, traffic, forwards
//   pulzr-lan policy <mac> parkeze|internet
//   pulzr-lan reserve <mac> <ip> [<name>]    the same address every time
//   pulzr-lan unreserve <mac>
//   pulzr-lan forward <ip> <port> <seconds> [<request id>]
//   pulzr-lan unforward <tunnel port> [<request id>]
//   pulzr-lan expose tcp|udp <port> <ip> [<client port>]
//   pulzr-lan unexpose tcp|udp <port>
//   pulzr-lan cellular <mac> allow|block [tcp:<port>[-<port>] udp:...]
//   pulzr-lan links                          which devices are which uplink
//   pulzr-lan seen <mac> <ip>                from a DHCP event
//   pulzr-lan save-usage                     each client's counts, kept
//                                              across the reboot to come
//   pulzr-lan isolate                        port isolation on every member
//   pulzr-lan address add|remove <a.b.c.d/nn>  another subnet on the bridge
//   pulzr-lan address for <device ip>          one for a device's own subnet
//   pulzr-lan scan                           which management ports each
//                                              client answers on (nmap)
//   pulzr-lan scan-service                   scan every so often (procd)
//
// ── THE TUNNEL FORWARD ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// From the management tunnel to one client's TCP port, for a bounded time.
// That is how somebody at Pulzar Cloud opens a PLC's own interface without the
// PLC holding a second connection or the site opening anything.
//
// It lives in a table of our own, inet pulzr_lan, not in fw4's. fw4 rebuilds
// its table on every reload, which would close a forward somebody is using
// and zero every client's counters whenever a WiFi network is joined. The
// expiry is the kernel's: each forward is an element with a timeout, so it
// closes on time with nothing on the gateway having to remember to close it,
// and a reboot closes them all. The guard chain drops a forwarded connection
// the moment its port leaves the open set, so a session already running does
// not outlive its window either.
//
// fw4's mgmt zone forwards nothing, so one rule is added to it
// (/usr/share/pulzr/lan/mgmt-forward.nft): accept what this table marked.
// The mark is set only by the DNAT here, and the DNAT only matches an open
// port.
//
// Every open and close is journalled to /etc/pulzr/lan/forwards.log, as a
// setup AP raise is, and `status` carries the recent entries to Pulzar Cloud.
//
// ── EXPOSED PORTS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// A port on the uplink's own address, forwarded to one client for good: a
// camera or a PLC a customer serves to the internet themselves, through their
// own router's forward to this gateway and their own DNS name (pulzr-dns's
// ddns). Kept in UCI (pulzr.expose_*), loaded into this table by apply.
//
// Only the wired uplinks and the WiFi uplink. Matched on device AND address
// (uplink_in), not on fw4's wan zone, because that zone also holds usbwan, a
// cellular dongle, and the console's addresses on the same port. The tunnel
// never reaches an exposed port, and a connection through one is dropped
// rather than leave on cellular. That is the line: a customer's traffic
// never rides our tunnel or our SIM.
//
// The client must hold a reservation, and keeps it while it is exposed: an
// exposed port to a dynamic address lands on whatever device leases it next.
// Ports the gateway itself answers on its uplink (RESERVED) are refused.
//
// ponytail: replies follow the main routing table. An exposed port answers on
// whichever wired or WiFi uplink carries the default route; arriving on
// another, the reply leaves the wrong way and the connection fails. Mark the
// connection with its uplink and route the reply by mark if a site ever
// needs more than one at once.
//
// fw4's wan zone forwards nothing, so one rule is added to it
// (/usr/share/pulzr/lan/wan-forward.nft), as for the tunnel's.
//
// ── CELLULAR ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Per client: allowed onto cellular, blocked from it, or allowed except some
// ports. A camera on the internet policy otherwise fails over onto the SIM
// with everything else and spends its data in a day. Matched on the cellular
// devices (wwan, usbwan) by MAC, before fw4 sees the packet, so it holds
// whatever the policy allows. REJECT, as the zone does: a client refused
// fails at once rather than retrying into a link it may not use.
//
// ── MORE SUBNETS ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// A device with a static address on another subnet (a camera set to
// 10.0.0.112 at the factory) is on the bridge but unreachable: the gateway
// has no address there. `address add 10.0.0.2/24` gives it one, as a second
// netifd interface on br-devlan (devlan_extra, `list ipaddr` in CIDR), the
// way the console's addresses share the uplink port. Not on network.devlan
// itself: 25-pulzr-lan rebuilds that section on every upgrade, and every
// reader of its ipaddr/netmask expects one address.
//
// Nothing else changes for those devices. Every rule here and in fw4's
// devlan zone matches the bridge device, not a subnet, so the zone's policy,
// isolation, the cellular rules and the counting hold for them as they do
// for a leased client; clientIp() accepts their addresses, so a forward, a
// reservation (the name resolves; dnsmasq leases nothing outside its range)
// and an exposed port reach them. No DHCP is served there.
//
// ── WHAT A DEVICE SAYS ON THE WIRE ─────────────────────────────────────────
//
// A device with a static address on a subnet the gateway has no address on
// is invisible to it: the kernel drops ARP from a foreign subnet, so it never
// reaches the neighbour table and `status` could list it by MAC only. Its own
// ARP says its address anyway (who-has its router, tell 10.0.0.112), so a
// bridge-family table of our own (bridge pulzr_lan_learn) reads every ARP
// arriving on a LAN port and keeps the sender's MAC and address in a set with
// a timeout. Passive: nothing is sent, no subnet is swept. ARP probes from
// 0.0.0.0 (a DHCP client checking its lease) are skipped.
//
// A learned address is reported and never acted on. It makes no route and no
// subnet; `address for <ip>` does, when somebody asks (the console's Add on
// the client's row). That picks the gateway's own address in the device's
// /24 from .253 down, skipping the device, any address seen on the wire and
// any that answers a duplicate-address probe (arping -D), so a silent device
// is the only collision left, and returns it. A /24, because factory
// defaults are /24 and a device on a wider subnet still reaches a /24 inside
// it; .254 and .1 are left alone, being where a site's router usually is.
//
// ── THE PORT SCAN ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Which management ports each client answers on: SSH, telnet and web, so the
// console offers a terminal or a web page only where one will open. nmap,
// a TCP connect to each port, polite timing (-T2: one probe at a time, 0.4 s
// apart), one retry, and only to the clients the gateway can see on the
// devlan bridge right now (a complete ARP entry), never a sweep of the
// subnet and never anything on an uplink. Every fifteen minutes and soon
// after a new lease, and not at all while the default route is cellular: the
// tunnel is not there to use what it finds, and the push would be billed.
// A scan that finds what the last one did pushes nothing.
//
// The result is in /var/run, so a reboot forgets it and the first scan after
// boot says it again; `status` carries each client's open ports.
//
// ── WHAT A CLIENT REACHES ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// The internet, by default, straight out whichever uplink is routing,
// masqueraded: never through the management tunnel or a relay. Pulzar meters
// it (below) and a plan prices it. `pulzr-lan policy <mac> parkeze` narrows
// one client to Pulzar, NTP and DNS, which is what every client got before
// metering existed.
//
// ── COUNTING ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Per client and per uplink, by nftables: a dynamic set keyed on the client's
// address and the uplink device, with a counter on each element. Counted after
// fw4 has filtered, so a blocked attempt is not traffic. nlbwmon counts per
// client but not per uplink, and per uplink is the question: which client
// spent the SIM's data, and which bytes a plan's allowance is spent on.
//
// The kernel counts per address since the table was made; `status` reports
// per MAC, as running totals within an epoch (`usageEpoch`), and Pulzar Cloud
// adds up the differences between reports. Three things keep a total from
// going backwards inside an epoch, which the cloud would otherwise have to
// guess about:
//
//   a reboot      the init script's stop writes every client's totals to
//                 flash (save-usage), and the table made at the next boot
//                 starts from them under the same epoch. One write per
//                 shutdown, never on a timer. A power cut writes nothing, so
//                 the next boot is a new epoch and what moved since the
//                 cloud's last poll is lost: minutes, not a month.
//   a new address the DHCP event that moves a MAC to another address, or an
//                 address to another MAC, folds the old address's counters
//                 into its owner's base and deletes them from the kernel, so
//                 nobody inherits anybody else's bytes.
//   a new table   a table remade by hand is a new epoch.

let fs = require('fs');
let uci = require('uci');

const TABLE = 'pulzr_lan';
const MARK = 0x504b;
const EXPOSE_MARK = 0x5045;
// What the gateway answers on its own uplink address: DHCP renewals, and the
// console, its WiFi sign-in proxy and SSH, which a site may reach it on.
const RESERVED = { tcp: [22, 80, 443, 8443], udp: [67, 68] };
const MAX_EXPOSED = 32;
const MAX_BLOCKS = 16;
// Which netifd interfaces are which, as /usr/share/pulzr/wired.sh and
// cellular.sh have them: wan and wan_<device> are wired (pulzr-ports),
// wwifi the WiFi uplink, wwan the modem and usbwan a dongle or a phone.
const CELLULAR_UPLINKS = ['wwan', 'usbwan'];
let exposeUplink = (name) => name == 'wwifi' || name == 'wan' || index(name, 'wan_') == 0;
// The tunnel-side ports a forward is reached on. Sixteen at once is far more
// than anybody opens; a full range is refused rather than grown.
const FIRST_PORT = 40000;
const LAST_PORT = 40015;
const MAX_SECONDS = 86400;
const STATE = '/etc/pulzr/lan';
const JOURNAL = STATE + '/forwards.log';
const SEEN = STATE + '/seen.json';
// A client's DHCP contact is kept a week after the last, then forgotten, so
// the visitor of two weeks ago neither stays on every report nor fills the
// MQTT agent's 8 KB. Pulzar Cloud keeps its own record of it. A reserved
// client keeps its entry: it is configured, not passing through.
const SEEN_KEEP = 7 * 86400;
const WIRE_ONLY_MAX = 8;
const LEARN = 'pulzr_lan_learn';
const PROBES = 8;
const EXTRA = 'devlan_extra';
const MAX_EXTRA = 8;
// Subnets another subnet on the bridge may not touch even while they are
// down: the console's, link-local, the setup AP's and the USB port's.
const FIXED_NETS = ['192.168.61.1/24', '169.254.0.1/16', '192.168.62.1/24', '192.168.63.1/24'];
const JOURNAL_KEEP = 200;
const JOURNAL_REPORT = 20;
const LEASES = '/tmp/dhcp.leases';
const ARP = '/proc/net/arp';
const SCAN_DIR = '/var/run/pulzr';
const SCAN = SCAN_DIR + '/lan-scan.json';
// What the console has a button for: SSH, telnet, and a web UI on the usual
// four. A device's other services are its own business.
const SCAN_PORTS = [22, 23, 80, 443, 8080, 8443];
const SCAN_EVERY = 900;
// The soonest a new lease is scanned again after the last scan.
const SCAN_SOONEST = 120;
// A client gone this long loses what was found on it.
const SCAN_KEEP = 7 * 86400;
const MAX_SCAN_HOSTS = 64;
const AGENT = '/usr/sbin/pulzr-mqtt-agent';
// The epoch and each client's base are in RAM; the flash copy exists only
// between a clean shutdown and the boot after it.
const USAGE_DIR = SCAN_DIR + '/lan-usage';
const USAGE_EPOCH = USAGE_DIR + '/epoch';
const USAGE_BASE = USAGE_DIR + '/base.json';
const USAGE_SAVED = STATE + '/usage.json';
const BOOT_ID = '/proc/sys/kernel/random/boot_id';

let cursor = uci.cursor();

let die = (msg, code) => {
    warn(msg, '\n');
    exit(code ?? 1);
};

let log = (msg) => system(['logger', '-t', 'lan', '-p', 'daemon.notice', msg]);

// Output of a fixed command, never a shell: every argument is its own argv
// entry, so nothing a caller passed can become part of a command.
let capture = (argv) => {
    let quoted = join(' ', map(argv, (a) => "'" + replace(a, "'", "'\\''") + "'"));
    let proc = fs.popen(quoted + ' 2>/dev/null', 'r');
    if (!proc) return null;
    let out = proc.read('all');
    proc.close();
    return out;
};

let captureJson = (argv) => {
    let out = capture(argv);
    if (!out) return null;
    try { return json(out); } catch (e) { return null; }
};

let nftScript = (text) => {
    let path = sprintf('/tmp/pulzr-lan.%d.nft', time());
    fs.writefile(path, text);
    let rc = system(['nft', '-f', path]);
    fs.unlink(path);
    return rc == 0;
};

// ── addresses ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

let ipToInt = (ip) => {
    let m = match(ip ?? '', /^([0-9]{1,3})\.([0-9]{1,3})\.([0-9]{1,3})\.([0-9]{1,3})$/);
    if (!m) return null;
    let n = 0;
    for (let i = 1; i <= 4; i++) {
        let b = int(m[i]);
        if (b > 255) return null;
        n = n * 256 + b;
    }
    return n;
};

let normalMac = (mac) => {
    mac = lc(mac ?? '');
    return match(mac, /^[0-9a-f]{2}(:[0-9a-f]{2}){5}$/) ? mac : null;
};

let macId = (mac) => replace(mac, /:/g, '');

let lanNet = () => {
    let addr = ipToInt(cursor.get('network', 'devlan', 'ipaddr'));
    let mask = ipToInt(cursor.get('network', 'devlan', 'netmask') ?? '255.255.255.0');
    if (addr == null || mask == null) return null;
    return { addr, mask, net: addr & mask, bcast: (addr & mask) | (~mask & 0xffffffff) };
};

// "10.0.0.2/24" as lanNet() has it, or null. A /31 or /32 has no room for a
// client, and nothing wider than /8 belongs on one bridge.
let cidr = (text) => {
    let m = match(text ?? '', /^([0-9.]+)\/([0-9]{1,2})$/);
    let addr = m ? ipToInt(m[1]) : null, bits = m ? int(m[2]) : 0;
    if (addr == null || bits < 8 || bits > 30) return null;
    let mask = (0xffffffff << (32 - bits)) & 0xffffffff;
    let net = addr & mask, bcast = net | (~mask & 0xffffffff);
    if (addr == net || addr == bcast) return null;
    return { addr, mask, bits, net, bcast,
             text: sprintf('%d.%d.%d.%d/%d', (addr >> 24) & 255, (addr >> 16) & 255, (addr >> 8) & 255, addr & 255, bits) };
};

let asList = (v) => type(v) == 'array' ? v : (v == null ? [] : [v]);

// The bridge's other subnets, as the gateway's own address on each.
let extraNets = () => filter(map(asList(cursor.get('network', EXTRA, 'ipaddr')), cidr), (n) => n);

// A client address: inside the LAN or one of its other subnets, and neither
// one of the gateway's addresses, a network nor a broadcast address.
let clientIp = (ip) => {
    let n = ipToInt(ip), lan = lanNet();
    if (n == null || !lan) return false;
    let nets = [lan, ...extraNets()];
    for (let x in nets)
        if (n == x.addr) return false;
    for (let x in nets)
        if ((n & x.mask) == x.net && n != x.net && n != x.bcast) return true;
    return false;
};

let bridge = () => cursor.get('network', 'devlan_dev', 'name') ?? 'br-devlan';

let intToIp = (n) => sprintf('%d.%d.%d.%d', (n >> 24) & 255, (n >> 16) & 255, (n >> 8) & 255, n & 255);

// The learning table, and which ports it reads: the bridge's members now.
// Refilled with the isolation flags, on every ifup and every port that
// comes back.
let learnTable = () => sprintf(`
table bridge ${LEARN} {
	set lan_ports { type ifname; }
	set wire4 { type ether_addr . ipv4_addr; flags dynamic, timeout; timeout 30m; size 512; }
	chain learn {
		type filter hook prerouting priority filter; policy accept;
		iifname @lan_ports ether type arp arp saddr ip != 0.0.0.0 update @wire4 { arp saddr ether . arp saddr ip }
	}
}
`);

let ensureLearn = () => {
    if (!length(capture(['nft', 'list', 'set', 'bridge', LEARN, 'wire4'])) && !nftScript(learnTable()))
        return false;
    let ports = map(cursor.get('network', 'devlan_dev', 'ports') ?? [], (p) => sprintf('"%s"', p));
    return nftScript(sprintf('flush set bridge %s lan_ports\n', LEARN) +
                     (length(ports) ? sprintf('add element bridge %s lan_ports { %s }\n', LEARN, join(', ', ports)) : ''));
};

// {mac: {ip, left}}: each device's address as its own ARP said it, the most
// recently heard where it said more than one.
let learned = () => {
    let out = {};
    let doc = captureJson(['nft', '-j', 'list', 'set', 'bridge', LEARN, 'wire4']);
    for (let item in doc?.nftables ?? [])
        for (let e in item.set?.elem ?? []) {
            let key = (e.elem ? e.elem.val : e)?.concat;
            let mac = normalMac(key?.[0]), ip = key?.[1];
            if (!mac || ipToInt(ip) == null) continue;
            let left = e.elem?.expires ?? 0;
            if (!out[mac] || left > out[mac].left) out[mac] = { ip, left };
        }
    return out;
};

// A client address, or the reason it is not one.
let needClient = (ip) => {
    if (clientIp(ip)) return;
    if (ipToInt(ip) == null) die('an IPv4 address', 2);
    for (let x in [lanNet(), ...extraNets()])
        if (ipToInt(ip) == x.addr || (ipToInt(ip) & x.mask) == x.net)
            die(sprintf('%s is the gateway\'s own or not a host address', ip), 2);
    die(sprintf('%s is on no subnet of the LAN: add one for it first', ip), 3);
};


// ── the table ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

let table = () => sprintf(`
table inet ${TABLE} {
	set usage_up { type ipv4_addr . ifname; flags dynamic; counter; }
	set usage_down { type ipv4_addr . ifname; flags dynamic; counter; }
	set forward_open { type inet_service; flags timeout; }
	map forward_to { type inet_service : ipv4_addr . inet_service; flags timeout; }
	set uplink_in { type ifname . ipv4_addr; }
	set cellular_if { type ifname; }
	set expose_open { type inet_proto . inet_service; }
	map expose_to { type inet_proto . inet_service : ipv4_addr . inet_service; }
	set cell_deny { type ether_addr; }
	set cell_block { type ether_addr . inet_proto . inet_service; flags interval; }

	chain dstnat {
		type nat hook prerouting priority dstnat - 5; policy accept;
		iifname "wg0" tcp dport @forward_open ct mark set 0x%x dnat ip to tcp dport map @forward_to
		iifname . ip daddr @uplink_in meta l4proto . th dport @expose_open ct mark set 0x%x dnat ip to meta l4proto . th dport map @expose_to
	}
	chain srcnat {
		type nat hook postrouting priority srcnat - 5; policy accept;
		ct mark 0x%x oifname "%s" masquerade
	}
	chain guard {
		type filter hook forward priority filter - 5; policy accept;
		ct mark 0x%x ct original proto-dst != @forward_open drop
		ct mark 0x%x meta l4proto . ct original proto-dst != @expose_open drop
		ct mark 0x%x oifname @cellular_if drop
		iifname "%s" oifname @cellular_if ether saddr @cell_deny reject
		iifname "%s" oifname @cellular_if ether saddr . meta l4proto . th dport @cell_block reject
	}
	chain account {
		type filter hook forward priority filter + 10; policy accept;
		iifname "%s" update @usage_up { ip saddr . oifname }
		oifname "%s" update @usage_down { ip daddr . iifname }
	}
}
`, MARK, EXPOSE_MARK, MARK, bridge(), MARK, EXPOSE_MARK, EXPOSE_MARK,
   bridge(), bridge(), bridge(), bridge());

// ── usage epochs ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

let readJson = (path) => {
    try { return json(fs.readfile(path) ?? 'null'); } catch (e) { return null; }
};

let writeJson = (path, doc) => {
    fs.writefile(path + '.tmp', sprintf('%J', doc));
    return fs.rename(path + '.tmp', path);
};

let bootId = () => trim(fs.readfile(BOOT_ID) ?? '');

let usageEpoch = () => {
    let epoch = trim(fs.readfile(USAGE_EPOCH) ?? '');
    if (length(epoch)) return epoch;
    // A table from before metering: its counters are real, but nothing was
    // reported under an epoch yet, so this one starts now.
    epoch = sprintf('%s-%x', substr(replace(bootId(), '-', ''), 0, 8) || 'boot', time());
    fs.mkdir(SCAN_DIR);
    fs.mkdir(USAGE_DIR);
    fs.writefile(USAGE_EPOCH, epoch + '\n');
    return epoch;
};

// A new table: the saved totals when the last boot ended cleanly, a new epoch
// otherwise. The flash copy goes either way, so a later power cut cannot
// bring back totals this boot has already counted past.
let startUsage = () => {
    let saved = readJson(USAGE_SAVED);
    if (fs.access(USAGE_SAVED)) fs.unlink(USAGE_SAVED);
    let restore = type(saved?.epoch) == 'string' && type(saved?.clients) == 'object' &&
                  saved.boot && saved.boot != bootId();
    fs.mkdir(SCAN_DIR);
    fs.mkdir(USAGE_DIR);
    fs.unlink(USAGE_EPOCH);
    if (restore) fs.writefile(USAGE_EPOCH, saved.epoch + '\n');
    writeJson(USAGE_BASE, restore ? saved.clients : {});
    log(restore ? sprintf('usage: %d client(s) carried over in epoch %s', length(saved.clients), saved.epoch)
                : sprintf('usage: new epoch %s', usageEpoch()));
};

// A table from before exposed ports has none of their sets. A gateway gets a
// new one at the reboot an upgrade brings; this is for a package swapped in
// by hand, and it closes any tunnel forward open at the time.
let ensureTable = () => {
    if (length(capture(['nft', 'list', 'set', 'inet', TABLE, 'uplink_in']))) return true;
    if (length(capture(['nft', 'list', 'table', 'inet', TABLE])))
        system(['nft', 'delete', 'table', 'inet', TABLE]);
    if (!nftScript(table())) return false;
    startUsage();
    return true;
};

// ── policy ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// internet is the default and has no rules of its own: 25-pulzr-lan's
// devlan_inet_<zone> accepts everything from the zone, after the rules that
// let every client reach Pulzar and NTP. parkeze is one REJECT per client per
// uplink zone, matched on its MAC so a new lease does not lose it, and it
// only works between the two: after Pulzar's accepts and before the zone's.
// fw4 matches in UCI order, and 25-pulzr-lan re-adds its rules at the end on
// every upgrade, so each apply puts these back where they belong.
//
// Compat: before metering the default was parkeze, and internet was an
// ACCEPT per client (lan_inet_*). Those go at the first apply.

let UPLINK_ZONES = ['wan', 'cellular'];

let ruleIndex = () => {
    let out = {};
    cursor.foreach('firewall', 'rule', (s) => out[s['.name']] = s['.index']);
    return out;
};

// Each Pulzar-only rule below its zone's Pulzar and NTP accepts and above its
// zone-wide one. True when anything moved.
let placeRules = (names) => {
    let moved = false;
    for (let name in names) {
        let zone = cursor.get('firewall', name, 'dest'), at = ruleIndex();
        let pulzr = at['devlan_pulzr_' + zone] ?? -1, ntp = at['devlan_ntp_' + zone] ?? -1;
        let floor = pulzr > ntp ? pulzr : ntp;
        let ceiling = at['devlan_inet_' + zone];
        if (ceiling == null || at[name] == null || (at[name] > floor && at[name] < ceiling)) continue;
        cursor.reorder('firewall', name, at[name] < ceiling ? ceiling - 1 : ceiling);
        moved = true;
    }
    return moved;
};

let applyPolicy = () => {
    let want = {};
    cursor.foreach('pulzr', 'lan_client', (s) => {
        let mac = normalMac(s.mac);
        if (!mac || s.policy != 'parkeze') return;
        for (let zone in UPLINK_ZONES)
            want[sprintf('lan_pulzr_%s_%s', macId(mac), zone)] = { mac, zone };
    });

    let have = {};
    cursor.foreach('firewall', 'rule', (s) => {
        if (index(s['.name'], 'lan_pulzr_') == 0 || index(s['.name'], 'lan_inet_') == 0)
            have[s['.name']] = { mac: s.src_mac, zone: s.dest };
    });

    let changed = false;
    for (let name in keys(have)) {
        if (want[name]?.mac == have[name].mac && want[name]?.zone == have[name].zone) continue;
        cursor.delete('firewall', name);
        changed = true;
    }
    for (let name in keys(want)) {
        if (have[name]?.mac == want[name].mac && have[name]?.zone == want[name].zone) continue;
        cursor.set('firewall', name, 'rule');
        cursor.set('firewall', name, 'name', 'LAN-Pulzar-only-' + macId(want[name].mac));
        cursor.set('firewall', name, 'src', 'devlan');
        cursor.set('firewall', name, 'dest', want[name].zone);
        cursor.set('firewall', name, 'src_mac', want[name].mac);
        cursor.set('firewall', name, 'target', 'REJECT');
        changed = true;
    }
    if (placeRules(keys(want))) changed = true;
    if (changed) {
        cursor.commit('firewall');
        system('/etc/init.d/firewall reload >/dev/null 2>&1', 30000);
    }
    return changed;
};

// A client's section holds its policy and its cellular rules; it goes when
// neither differs from the default.
let saveClient = (mac, set) => {
    let name = 'lan_' + macId(mac);
    let s = cursor.get_all('pulzr', name) ?? {};
    set(s);
    if (s.policy != 'parkeze' && s.cellular != 'block' && !length(s.cellular_block ?? [])) {
        cursor.delete('pulzr', name);
    } else {
        cursor.set('pulzr', name, 'lan_client');
        cursor.set('pulzr', name, 'mac', mac);
        for (let opt in ['policy', 'cellular', 'cellular_block']) {
            if (s[opt] == null || (type(s[opt]) == 'array' && !length(s[opt])))
                cursor.delete('pulzr', name, opt);
            else
                cursor.set('pulzr', name, opt, s[opt]);
        }
    }
    cursor.commit('pulzr');
};

// "tcp:554" or "udp:5000-5010" as nft writes it, or null.
let blockEntry = (text) => {
    let m = match(text ?? '', /^(tcp|udp):([0-9]{1,5})(-([0-9]{1,5}))?$/);
    if (!m) return null;
    let lo = int(m[2]), hi = m[4] ? int(m[4]) : lo;
    if (lo < 1 || hi > 65535 || hi < lo) return null;
    return { proto: m[1], ports: lo == hi ? sprintf('%d', lo) : sprintf('%d-%d', lo, hi) };
};

let exposed = () => {
    let out = [];
    cursor.foreach('pulzr', 'lan_expose', (s) =>
        push(out, { proto: s.proto, port: int(s.port), ip: s.ip, dport: int(s.dport) }));
    return sort(out, (a, b) => a.port - b.port || (a.proto < b.proto ? -1 : 1));
};

// The uplinks by role, from netifd: the wired and WiFi uplinks' devices and
// addresses, which exposed ports answer on, and the cellular devices.
let links = () => {
    let out = { expose: [], cellular: [] };
    let dump = captureJson(['ubus', 'call', 'network.interface', 'dump']);
    for (let ifc in dump?.interface ?? []) {
        let dev = ifc.l3_device;
        if (!ifc.up || !dev) continue;
        if (exposeUplink(ifc.interface))
            for (let a in ifc['ipv4-address'] ?? [])
                push(out.expose, { uplink: ifc.interface, device: dev, address: a.address });
        if (ifc.interface in CELLULAR_UPLINKS)
            push(out.cellular, dev);
    }
    return out;
};

// Every set this table loads from state, in one transaction: a flush and its
// refill are atomic, so nothing is ever briefly open or briefly unguarded.
let loadSets = () => {
    let l = links(), lines = [];
    let fill = (set, elems) => {
        push(lines, sprintf('flush set inet %s %s', TABLE, set));
        if (length(elems)) push(lines, sprintf('add element inet %s %s { %s }', TABLE, set, join(', ', elems)));
    };
    fill('uplink_in', map(l.expose, (e) => sprintf('"%s" . %s', e.device, e.address)));
    fill('cellular_if', map(uniq(l.cellular), (d) => sprintf('"%s"', d)));

    let open = [], to = [];
    for (let e in exposed()) {
        push(open, sprintf('%s . %d', e.proto, e.port));
        push(to, sprintf('%s . %d : %s . %d', e.proto, e.port, e.ip, e.dport));
    }
    fill('expose_open', open);
    push(lines, sprintf('flush map inet %s expose_to', TABLE));
    if (length(to)) push(lines, sprintf('add element inet %s expose_to { %s }', TABLE, join(', ', to)));

    let deny = [], block = [];
    cursor.foreach('pulzr', 'lan_client', (s) => {
        let mac = normalMac(s.mac);
        if (!mac) return;
        if (s.cellular == 'block') push(deny, mac);
        else for (let text in s.cellular_block ?? []) {
            let b = blockEntry(text);
            if (b) push(block, sprintf('%s . %s . %s', mac, b.proto, b.ports));
        }
    });
    fill('cell_deny', deny);
    fill('cell_block', block);
    return nftScript(join('\n', lines) + '\n');
};

// ── what a client is ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

let readSeen = () => {
    try { return json(fs.readfile(SEEN) ?? '{}') ?? {}; } catch (e) { return {}; }
};

let writeSeen = (seen) => {
    fs.mkdir(STATE);
    fs.writefile(SEEN + '.tmp', sprintf('%J', seen));
    fs.rename(SEEN + '.tmp', SEEN);
};

let leases = () => {
    let out = [];
    for (let line in split(fs.readfile(LEASES) ?? '', '\n')) {
        let f = split(trim(line), /\s+/);
        if (length(f) < 4 || !clientIp(f[2])) continue;
        push(out, { expires: int(f[0]), mac: normalMac(f[1]), ip: f[2], hostname: f[3] == '*' ? null : f[3] });
    }
    return out;
};

// Who is answering right now: a complete ARP entry on the bridge.
let online = () => {
    let out = {}, dev = bridge();
    for (let line in split(fs.readfile(ARP) ?? '', '\n')) {
        let f = split(trim(line), /\s+/);
        if (length(f) >= 6 && f[5] == dev && f[2] == '0x2')
            out[normalMac(f[3])] = f[0];
    }
    return out;
};

// The port each client is behind, from the bridge's own forwarding table.
//
// Not `bridge fdb show`: that also dumps every DSA port's hardware table, and
// on the GL-SFT1200 the mxl-gsw1xx switch answers over MDIO at about six
// seconds a port, holding RTNL the whole time. The console's LAN page took
// twelve seconds, and every request that touched the network behind it (wg,
// ubus network) waited too. The bridge learns the same client addresses in
// software from the frames that reach the CPU, which is every client that has
// asked for a lease, and brforward hands that table over without touching the
// switch: 16 bytes an entry, the MAC, then the port number, then is_local.
let ports = () => {
    let out = {}, dev = bridge(), byNumber = {};
    let dir = sprintf('/sys/class/net/%s/brif', dev);
    for (let name in fs.lsdir(dir) ?? [])
        byNumber[int(trim(fs.readfile(sprintf('%s/%s/port_no', dir, name)) ?? ''), 16)] = name;
    let table = fs.readfile(sprintf('/sys/class/net/%s/brforward', dev)) ?? '';
    for (let at = 0; at + 16 <= length(table); at += 16) {
        if (ord(table, at + 7)) continue;
        let number = ord(table, at + 6) | (ord(table, at + 12) << 8);
        let mac = join(':', map([0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5], (i) => sprintf('%02x', ord(table, at + i))));
        if (byNumber[number]) out[mac] ??= byNumber[number];
    }
    return out;
};

// Each LAN port's link, from sysfs: no RTNL dump, so it costs what reading
// six small files does. speed and duplex only with carrier, since the kernel
// refuses them on a port without one. carrierChanges counts every up and
// down since boot (a port that came up once at boot reads 1 or 2), so a
// cable or a device that keeps dropping shows as a number that keeps moving.
let portLinks = (ports) => map(ports, (name) => {
    let sys = sprintf('/sys/class/net/%s/', name);
    let read = (f) => trim(fs.readfile(sys + f) ?? '');
    let up = read('carrier') == '1';
    let speed = up ? int(read('speed')) : 0;
    let duplex = up ? read('duplex') : '';
    return { name, up, speed: speed > 0 ? speed : null,
             duplex: duplex == 'full' || duplex == 'half' ? duplex : null,
             carrierChanges: int(read('carrier_changes')) || 0 };
});

// Device to the logical uplink it carries, so traffic reads "wan", "wwan"
// and "wwifi" rather than eth0 and phy0-sta0. The uplink port also carries
// the console and link-local interfaces, so the one with a default route
// names it.
let uplinkNames = () => {
    let out = {};
    let dump = captureJson(['ubus', 'call', 'network.interface', 'dump']);
    let routes = (ifc) => length(filter(ifc.route ?? [], (r) => r.target == '0.0.0.0' && r.mask == 0));
    for (let ifc in sort(dump?.interface ?? [], (a, b) => routes(b) - routes(a)))
        if (ifc.l3_device && ifc.interface != 'devlan' && ifc.interface != 'loopback')
            out[ifc.l3_device] ??= ifc.interface;
    // A forward's replies, which netifd does not know as an interface.
    out.wg0 = 'tunnel';
    return out;
};

let counters = (set, names) => {
    let out = {};
    let doc = captureJson(['nft', '-j', 'list', 'set', 'inet', TABLE, set]);
    for (let item in doc?.nftables ?? []) {
        for (let e in item.set?.elem ?? []) {
            let key = e.elem?.val?.concat, c = e.elem?.counter;
            if (!key || !c) continue;
            let uplink = names[key[1]] ?? key[1];
            out[key[0]] ??= {};
            out[key[0]][uplink] ??= { bytes: 0, packets: 0 };
            out[key[0]][uplink].bytes += c.bytes;
            out[key[0]][uplink].packets += c.packets;
        }
    }
    return out;
};

// ── usage ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

let addTraffic = (into, from) => {
    for (let uplink in keys(from ?? {})) {
        into[uplink] ??= { txBytes: 0, rxBytes: 0 };
        into[uplink].txBytes += from[uplink].txBytes ?? 0;
        into[uplink].rxBytes += from[uplink].rxBytes ?? 0;
    }
    return into;
};

// {ip: {uplink: {txBytes, rxBytes}}} from both sets.
let kernelTraffic = (names) => {
    let up = counters('usage_up', names), down = counters('usage_down', names), out = {};
    for (let ip in uniq([...keys(up), ...keys(down)])) {
        out[ip] = {};
        for (let uplink in uniq([...keys(up[ip] ?? {}), ...keys(down[ip] ?? {})]))
            out[ip][uplink] = { txBytes: up[ip]?.[uplink]?.bytes ?? 0, rxBytes: down[ip]?.[uplink]?.bytes ?? 0 };
    }
    return out;
};

// What an address counted, moved into its owner's base and out of the
// kernel. Deleted first and written second, so a status read in between
// reports a little less, never twice: the next report makes it up.
let foldAddress = (ip, mac) => {
    let names = uplinkNames(), counted = kernelTraffic(names)[ip];
    if (!counted) return;
    let lines = [];
    for (let set in ['usage_up', 'usage_down']) {
        let doc = captureJson(['nft', '-j', 'list', 'set', 'inet', TABLE, set]);
        for (let item in doc?.nftables ?? [])
            for (let e in item.set?.elem ?? []) {
                let key = e.elem?.val?.concat;
                if (key && key[0] == ip)
                    push(lines, sprintf('delete element inet %s %s { %s . "%s" }', TABLE, set, ip, key[1]));
            }
    }
    if (length(lines)) nftScript(join('\n', lines) + '\n');
    let base = readJson(USAGE_BASE) ?? {};
    base[mac] = addTraffic(base[mac] ?? {}, counted);
    fs.mkdir(SCAN_DIR);
    fs.mkdir(USAGE_DIR);
    writeJson(USAGE_BASE, base);
};

// {mac: {uplink: {txBytes, rxBytes}}}: each client's running totals in this
// epoch, for the clients whose address is known.
let usage = (ipOf) => {
    let base = readJson(USAGE_BASE) ?? {}, kernel = kernelTraffic(uplinkNames()), out = {};
    for (let mac in uniq([...keys(base), ...keys(ipOf)]))
        out[mac] = addTraffic(addTraffic({}, base[mac]), ipOf[mac] ? kernel[ipOf[mac]] : null);
    return out;
};

let forwards = () => {
    let out = [];
    let doc = captureJson(['nft', '-j', 'list', 'map', 'inet', TABLE, 'forward_to']);
    for (let item in doc?.nftables ?? []) {
        for (let e in item.map?.elem ?? []) {
            let key = e[0]?.elem, to = e[1]?.concat;
            if (!key || !to) continue;
            push(out, { port: key.val, ip: to[0], dport: to[1], expiresIn: key.expires });
        }
    }
    return out;
};

let journalTail = () => {
    let lines = filter(split(fs.readfile(JOURNAL) ?? '', '\n'), (l) => length(l));
    let out = [];
    for (let line in slice(lines, -JOURNAL_REPORT)) {
        try { push(out, json(line)); } catch (e) { }
    }
    return out;
};

let journal = (entry) => {
    fs.mkdir(STATE);
    let lines = filter(split(fs.readfile(JOURNAL) ?? '', '\n'), (l) => length(l));
    push(lines, sprintf('%J', entry));
    fs.writefile(JOURNAL + '.tmp', join('\n', slice(lines, -JOURNAL_KEEP)) + '\n');
    fs.rename(JOURNAL + '.tmp', JOURNAL);
};

// ── the port scan ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

let readScan = () => {
    try { return json(fs.readfile(SCAN) ?? '{}') ?? {}; } catch (e) { return {}; }
};

// Whether a scan's push would ride cellular, or go nowhere: the interface
// holding the lowest-metric default route.
let onCellular = () => {
    let dump = captureJson(['ubus', 'call', 'network.interface', 'dump']), best = null;
    for (let ifc in dump?.interface ?? []) {
        if (!ifc.up) continue;
        for (let r in ifc.route ?? [])
            if (r.target == '0.0.0.0' && r.mask == 0 && (best == null || (r.metric ?? 0) < best.metric))
                best = { metric: r.metric ?? 0, name: ifc.interface };
    }
    return best == null || (best.name in CELLULAR_UPLINKS);
};

// `Host: 192.168.64.20 ()	Ports: 22/open/tcp//ssh///, 80/closed/tcp//http///`
// from nmap -oG, as {ip: [open ports]}. null when nmap did not finish: a scan
// that did not run says nothing about what is closed.
let parseScan = (text) => {
    if (!match(text ?? '', /# Nmap done/)) return null;
    let out = {};
    for (let line in split(text, '\n')) {
        let m = match(line, /^Host: ([0-9.]+) [^\t]*\tPorts: ([^\t]*)/);
        if (!m) continue;
        let open = out[m[1]] ??= [];
        for (let entry in split(m[2], ', ')) {
            let f = split(trim(entry), '/');
            let port = int(f[0]);
            if (f[1] == 'open' && f[2] == 'tcp' && (port in SCAN_PORTS) && !(port in open)) push(open, port);
        }
    }
    for (let ip in keys(out)) out[ip] = sort(out[ip], (a, b) => a - b);
    return out;
};

// The leases as a scan saw them, MAC and address only: a new one is a client
// worth scanning before the fifteen minutes are up.
let leaseMark = () => join(' ', sort(map(leases(), (l) => l.mac + '=' + l.ip)));

let scan = () => {
    if (!lanNet() || onCellular()) return null;
    let targets = {};
    let now = online();
    for (let mac in keys(now))
        if (normalMac(mac) && clientIp(now[mac]) && length(targets) < MAX_SCAN_HOSTS) targets[now[mac]] = mac;
    let at = time(), was = readScan(), clients = {};
    for (let mac in keys(was.clients ?? {}))
        if (at - (was.clients[mac].at ?? 0) < SCAN_KEEP) clients[mac] = was.clients[mac];
    if (length(targets)) {
        let found = parseScan(capture(['nmap', '-n', '-Pn', '-sT', '-T2', '--max-retries', '1',
                                       '--host-timeout', '60s', '-e', bridge(),
                                       '-p', join(',', SCAN_PORTS), '-oG', '-', ...keys(targets)]));
        if (found == null) return null;
        for (let ip in keys(targets))
            clients[targets[ip]] = { ip, open: found[ip] ?? [], at };
    }
    let doc = { at, leases: leaseMark(), clients };
    fs.mkdir(SCAN_DIR);
    fs.writefile(SCAN + '.tmp', sprintf('%J', doc));
    fs.rename(SCAN + '.tmp', SCAN);
    let same = (a, b) => sprintf('%J', map(sort(keys(a ?? {})), (m) => [m, a[m].open])) ==
                         sprintf('%J', map(sort(keys(b ?? {})), (m) => [m, b[m].open]));
    return { doc, changed: !same(was.clients, clients) };
};

let pushLan = () => {
    if (cursor.get('pulzr', 'adoption', 'state') == 'adopted' && fs.access(AGENT, 'x'))
        system([AGENT, '--push', 'lan']);
};

let status = () => {
    let lan = lanNet();
    if (!lan) return { configured: false };

    let clients = {};
    let client = (mac) => (clients[mac] ??= { mac, ip: null, hostname: null, reserved: null,
                                              policy: 'internet', cellular: 'allow', cellularBlock: [],
                                              online: false,
                                              firstSeen: null, lastSeen: null, traffic: {} });

    for (let l in leases()) {
        if (!l.mac) continue;
        let c = client(l.mac);
        c.ip = l.ip;
        c.hostname = l.hostname;
        c.leaseExpires = l.expires;
    }
    cursor.foreach('dhcp', 'host', (s) => {
        let mac = normalMac(type(s.mac) == 'array' ? s.mac[0] : s.mac);
        if (!mac || index(s['.name'], 'lan_') != 0) return;
        let c = client(mac);
        c.reserved = s.ip;
        c.hostname ??= s.name;
        c.ip ??= s.ip;
    });
    cursor.foreach('pulzr', 'lan_client', (s) => {
        let mac = normalMac(s.mac);
        if (!mac) return;
        let c = client(mac);
        if (s.policy == 'parkeze') c.policy = 'parkeze';
        if (s.cellular == 'block') c.cellular = 'block';
        c.cellularBlock = filter(s.cellular_block ?? [], (t) => blockEntry(t));
    });
    let seen = readSeen(), now = online(), at = time();
    for (let mac in keys(seen)) {
        if (at - (seen[mac].last ?? 0) > SEEN_KEEP && !clients[mac]) continue;
        let c = client(mac);
        c.firstSeen = seen[mac].first;
        c.lastSeen = seen[mac].last;
    }
    for (let mac in keys(now)) {
        let c = client(mac);
        c.online = true;
        c.ip ??= now[mac];
        c.lastSeen = time();
    }

    // On the wire but never in DHCP or ARP: a camera with a static address
    // on another subnet sends frames the bridge learns and nothing else.
    // Listed with its port and nothing more, so the cloud can say it is
    // there. Capped: a LAN port cabled into a site switch learns every MAC
    // on that network, and the whole answer has to stay under the MQTT
    // agent's 8 KB.
    let unknown = 0;

    // What each device's own ARP says its address is (see WHAT A DEVICE SAYS
    // ON THE WIRE). A client with no lease, reservation or neighbour entry
    // takes it as its address, marked learned, and onLan says whether any
    // subnet of the LAN holds it: the console offers to add one when not.
    let wire = learned();
    for (let mac in sort(keys(wire))) {
        if (!clients[mac] && unknown++ >= WIRE_ONLY_MAX) continue;
        let c = client(mac);
        if (c.ip) continue;
        c.ip = wire[mac].ip;
        c.learned = true;
        c.onLan = clientIp(c.ip);
    }

    let behind = ports();
    for (let mac in sort(keys(behind)))
        if (!clients[mac] && unknown++ < WIRE_ONLY_MAX) client(mac);
    for (let mac in keys(clients))
        clients[mac].port = behind[mac] ?? null;

    // A client the last scans reached carries the ports it answered on; one
    // no scan has reached carries none, which is unknown, not closed.
    let scanned = readScan();
    for (let mac in keys(scanned.clients ?? {}))
        if (clients[mac]) clients[mac].services = scanned.clients[mac].open;

    let ipOf = {};
    for (let mac in keys(clients))
        if (clients[mac].ip) ipOf[mac] = clients[mac].ip;
    let totals = usage(ipOf);
    for (let mac in keys(clients))
        clients[mac].traffic = totals[mac] ?? {};

    return {
        configured: true,
        address: cursor.get('network', 'devlan', 'ipaddr'),
        ports: cursor.get('network', 'devlan_dev', 'ports') ?? [],
        links: portLinks(cursor.get('network', 'devlan_dev', 'ports') ?? []),
        clients: sort(values(clients), (a, b) => (a.ip ?? '') < (b.ip ?? '') ? -1 : 1),
        forwards: forwards(),
        exposed: exposed(),
        addresses: map(extraNets(), (n) => n.text),
        // Where exposed ports answer now: the wired and WiFi uplinks'
        // addresses. A private one is behind a site router, which has to
        // forward to it as well.
        exposedOn: map(links().expose, (e) => ({ uplink: e.uplink, address: e.address })),
        journal: journalTail(),
        scannedAt: readScan().at ?? null,
        usageEpoch: usageEpoch(),
    };
};

// Port isolation in the bridge, so clients on different ports reach each other
// only through the gateway, which forwards nothing between them. netifd has no
// option for it, so it is the kernel's flag, set on every ifup and every time
// a port comes back.
let isolate = () => {
    for (let port in cursor.get('network', 'devlan_dev', 'ports') ?? []) {
        let flag = sprintf('/sys/class/net/%s/brport/isolated', port);
        if (fs.access(flag)) fs.writefile(flag, '1');
    }
    ensureLearn() || warn('could not load the learning table\n');
};

// A reservation an exposed port points at stays where it is: moved or
// released, the port would follow whatever leases the address next.
let keepExposed = (was, now) => {
    if (!was || was == now) return;
    for (let e in exposed())
        if (e.ip == was) die(sprintf('%s has exposed ports; remove them first', was), 3);
};

// Whether two subnets share an address.
let overlap = (a, b) => {
    let mask = a.mask & b.mask;
    return (a.net & mask) == (b.net & mask);
};

// What a new subnet on the bridge may not overlap: the LAN, the others, the
// fixed networks and every subnet the gateway holds now (uplinks, tunnel).
let refuseExtra = (n) => {
    if (overlap(n, lanNet())) return 'overlaps the LAN';
    for (let x in extraNets())
        if (overlap(n, x)) return sprintf('overlaps %s', x.text);
    for (let f in FIXED_NETS)
        if (overlap(n, cidr(f))) return sprintf('overlaps %s', f);
    let dump = captureJson(['ubus', 'call', 'network.interface', 'dump']);
    for (let ifc in dump?.interface ?? []) {
        if (ifc.interface == 'devlan' || ifc.interface == EXTRA || ifc.interface == 'loopback') continue;
        for (let a in ifc['ipv4-address'] ?? []) {
            let x = cidr(sprintf('%s/%d', a.address, a.mask));
            if (x && overlap(n, x)) return sprintf('overlaps %s (%s)', x.text, ifc.interface);
        }
    }
    return null;
};

let saveExtra = (texts) => {
    if (length(texts)) {
        cursor.set('network', EXTRA, 'interface');
        cursor.set('network', EXTRA, 'device', bridge());
        cursor.set('network', EXTRA, 'proto', 'static');
        cursor.set('network', EXTRA, 'ipaddr', texts);
    } else {
        cursor.delete('network', EXTRA);
    }
    cursor.commit('network');
    system(['ubus', 'call', 'network', 'reload']);
};

// ── commands ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

let commands = {
    apply: () => {
        if (!lanNet()) die('no LAN on this gateway');
        // A start that finds the table already there followed a stop that
        // was not a shutdown: the counters running now are newer than what
        // that stop saved, which must never be restored. Only here: a
        // shutdown's ifdown hotplugs reach ensureTable after the save.
        if (length(capture(['nft', 'list', 'set', 'inet', TABLE, 'usage_up'])) && fs.access(USAGE_SAVED))
            fs.unlink(USAGE_SAVED);
        ensureTable() || die('could not load the nftables table');
        isolate();
        loadSets() || die('could not load the exposed ports and cellular rules');
        applyPolicy();
    },

    isolate: () => isolate(),

    // Another subnet on the bridge, for devices with static addresses.
    address: (op, text) => {
        lanNet() || die('no LAN on this gateway');
        if (op == 'for') {
            let device = ipToInt(text) ?? die('a device\'s IPv4 address', 2);
            clientIp(text) && die(sprintf('%s is already on the LAN', text), 3);
            let net = device & 0xffffff00, taken = {}, pick = null, probes = 0;
            let wire = learned();
            for (let mac in keys(wire)) taken[wire[mac].ip] = true;
            let why = refuseExtra(cidr(intToIp(net | 253) + '/24'));
            why && die(sprintf('%s/24 %s', intToIp(net), why), 3);
            for (let host = 253; host >= 2 && pick == null && probes < PROBES; host--) {
                let ip = intToIp(net | host);
                if ((net | host) == device || taken[ip]) continue;
                probes++;
                if (system(['arping', '-D', '-q', '-I', bridge(), '-c', '2', '-w', '2', ip]) == 0) pick = ip;
            }
            pick ?? die(sprintf('no free address in %s/24 after %d probes', intToIp(net), probes), 3);
            op = 'add';
            text = pick + '/24';
        }
        let n = cidr(text) ?? die('an address and prefix, like 10.0.0.2/24', 2);
        let have = extraNets();
        if (op == 'add') {
            if (length(filter(have, (x) => x.text == n.text))) {
                printf('%J\n', { addresses: map(have, (x) => x.text) });
                return;
            }
            (length(have) < MAX_EXTRA) || die(sprintf('at most %d other subnets', MAX_EXTRA), 3);
            let why = refuseExtra(n);
            why && die(sprintf('%s %s', n.text, why), 3);
            push(have, n);
        } else if (op == 'remove') {
            let kept = filter(have, (x) => x.text != n.text);
            (length(kept) < length(have)) || die(sprintf('%s is not on the LAN', n.text), 3);
            // An exposed port into it would point at nothing.
            for (let e in exposed())
                if ((ipToInt(e.ip) & n.mask) == n.net) die(sprintf('%s has exposed ports; remove them first', e.ip), 3);
            have = kept;
        } else {
            die('address add|remove <a.b.c.d/nn> | address for <device ip>', 2);
        }
        let texts = map(have, (x) => x.text);
        saveExtra(texts);
        log(sprintf('address %s %s', op, n.text));
        printf('%J\n', { addresses: texts, address: n.text });
    },

    // An uplink came up, went down or changed address (hotplug).
    links: () => {
        if (!lanNet()) exit(0);
        (ensureTable() && loadSets()) || die('could not load the uplinks');
    },

    status: () => printf('%J\n', status()),

    policy: (mac, policy) => {
        mac = normalMac(mac) ?? die('a MAC address, like 00:11:22:33:44:55', 2);
        // Compat: `parkeze` is the policy value the cloud sends and reads.
        (policy == 'parkeze' || policy == 'internet') || die('policy is parkeze or internet', 2);
        saveClient(mac, (s) => s.policy = policy == 'parkeze' ? policy : null);
        applyPolicy();
        log(sprintf('%s policy %s', mac, policy));
        printf('%J\n', { mac, policy });
    },

    cellular: (mac, mode, ...blocks) => {
        mac = normalMac(mac) ?? die('a MAC address, like 00:11:22:33:44:55', 2);
        (mode == 'allow' || mode == 'block') || die('cellular is allow or block', 2);
        (length(blocks) <= MAX_BLOCKS) || die(sprintf('at most %d blocked ports', MAX_BLOCKS), 2);
        for (let b in blocks)
            blockEntry(b) || die(sprintf('%s is not tcp:<port> or udp:<port>-<port>', b), 2);
        blocks = mode == 'allow' ? uniq(blocks) : [];
        saveClient(mac, (s) => {
            s.cellular = mode == 'block' ? 'block' : null;
            s.cellular_block = blocks;
        });
        ensureTable() && loadSets() || die('could not load the cellular rules');
        log(sprintf('%s cellular %s%s', mac, mode, length(blocks) ? ' except ' + join(' ', blocks) : ''));
        printf('%J\n', { mac, cellular: mode, cellularBlock: blocks });
    },

    expose: (proto, port, ip, dport) => {
        (proto == 'tcp' || proto == 'udp') || die('tcp or udp', 2);
        port = match(port ?? '', /^[0-9]{1,5}$/) ? int(port) : 0;
        (port >= 1 && port <= 65535) || die('a port from 1 to 65535', 2);
        (port in RESERVED[proto]) && die(sprintf('%s %d is the gateway\'s own', proto, port), 3);
        needClient(ip);
        dport = dport == null ? port : (match(dport, /^[0-9]{1,5}$/) ? int(dport) : 0);
        (dport >= 1 && dport <= 65535) || die('a client port from 1 to 65535', 2);
        let reserved = false;
        cursor.foreach('dhcp', 'host', (s) => {
            if (s.ip == ip && index(s['.name'], 'lan_') == 0) reserved = true;
        });
        reserved || die(sprintf('reserve %s first', ip), 3);
        let name = sprintf('expose_%s_%d', proto, port);
        (cursor.get('pulzr', name) || length(exposed()) < MAX_EXPOSED)
            || die(sprintf('at most %d exposed ports', MAX_EXPOSED), 3);
        cursor.set('pulzr', name, 'lan_expose');
        cursor.set('pulzr', name, 'proto', proto);
        cursor.set('pulzr', name, 'port', sprintf('%d', port));
        cursor.set('pulzr', name, 'ip', ip);
        cursor.set('pulzr', name, 'dport', sprintf('%d', dport));
        cursor.commit('pulzr');
        ensureTable() && loadSets() || die('could not load the exposed ports');
        journal({ at: time(), op: 'expose', proto, port, ip, dport });
        log(sprintf('exposed %s %d -> %s:%d', proto, port, ip, dport));
        printf('%J\n', { proto, port, ip, dport });
    },

    unexpose: (proto, port) => {
        let name = sprintf('expose_%s_%d', proto ?? '', int(port ?? 0));
        let found = cursor.get_all('pulzr', name) ?? die('nothing exposed on that port', 3);
        cursor.delete('pulzr', name);
        cursor.commit('pulzr');
        ensureTable() && loadSets() || die('could not load the exposed ports');
        journal({ at: time(), op: 'unexpose', proto, port: int(port), ip: found.ip, dport: int(found.dport) });
        log(sprintf('unexposed %s %d', proto, int(port)));
        printf('%J\n', { proto, port: int(port), closed: true });
    },

    reserve: (mac, ip, name) => {
        mac = normalMac(mac) ?? die('a MAC address, like 00:11:22:33:44:55', 2);
        needClient(ip);
        (name == null || match(name, /^[A-Za-z0-9-]{1,63}$/)) || die('a name is letters, digits and hyphens', 2);
        let section = 'lan_' + macId(mac);
        keepExposed(cursor.get('dhcp', section, 'ip'), ip);
        let taken = null;
        cursor.foreach('dhcp', 'host', (s) => {
            if (s.ip == ip && s['.name'] != section) taken = s['.name'];
        });
        taken && die(sprintf('%s is already reserved', ip), 3);
        cursor.set('dhcp', section, 'host');
        cursor.set('dhcp', section, 'mac', mac);
        cursor.set('dhcp', section, 'ip', ip);
        if (name) {
            cursor.set('dhcp', section, 'name', name);
            cursor.set('dhcp', section, 'dns', '1');
        } else {
            cursor.delete('dhcp', section, 'name');
            cursor.delete('dhcp', section, 'dns');
        }
        cursor.commit('dhcp');
        system('/etc/init.d/dnsmasq reload >/dev/null 2>&1', 30000);
        log(sprintf('%s reserved %s', mac, ip));
        printf('%J\n', { mac, ip, name });
    },

    unreserve: (mac) => {
        mac = normalMac(mac) ?? die('a MAC address, like 00:11:22:33:44:55', 2);
        keepExposed(cursor.get('dhcp', 'lan_' + macId(mac), 'ip'), null);
        cursor.delete('dhcp', 'lan_' + macId(mac));
        cursor.commit('dhcp');
        system('/etc/init.d/dnsmasq reload >/dev/null 2>&1', 30000);
        log(sprintf('%s reservation released', mac));
        printf('%J\n', { mac, ip: null });
    },

    forward: (ip, port, seconds, id) => {
        needClient(ip);
        port = match(port ?? '', /^[0-9]{1,5}$/) ? int(port) : 0;
        (port >= 1 && port <= 65535) || die('a port from 1 to 65535', 2);
        seconds = match(seconds ?? '', /^[0-9]{1,6}$/) ? int(seconds) : 0;
        (seconds >= 60 && seconds <= MAX_SECONDS) || die(sprintf('from 60 to %d seconds', MAX_SECONDS), 2);
        ensureTable() || die('could not load the nftables table');

        let open = {};
        for (let f in forwards()) open[f.port] = f;
        let tunnel = null;
        for (let p = FIRST_PORT; p <= LAST_PORT && tunnel == null; p++)
            if (!open[p]) tunnel = p;
        tunnel ?? die('every forward is in use', 3);

        // One transaction, so a port is never open without a target.
        nftScript(sprintf('add element inet %s forward_to { %d timeout %ds : %s . %d }\n' +
                          'add element inet %s forward_open { %d timeout %ds }\n',
                          TABLE, tunnel, seconds, ip, port, TABLE, tunnel, seconds))
            || die('could not open the forward');

        let at = time();
        journal({ at, op: 'open', port: tunnel, ip, dport: port, seconds, id });
        log(sprintf('forward %d -> %s:%d open for %ds', tunnel, ip, port, seconds));
        printf('%J\n', { port: tunnel, ip, dport: port, expires: at + seconds });
    },

    unforward: (port, id) => {
        port = match(port ?? '', /^[0-9]{1,5}$/) ? int(port) : 0;
        let found = null;
        for (let f in forwards()) if (f.port == port) found = f;
        found ?? die('no forward on that port', 3);
        nftScript(sprintf('delete element inet %s forward_open { %d }\n' +
                          'delete element inet %s forward_to { %d }\n',
                          TABLE, port, TABLE, port))
            || die('could not close the forward');
        journal({ at: time(), op: 'close', port, ip: found.ip, dport: found.dport, id });
        log(sprintf('forward %d -> %s:%d closed', port, found.ip, found.dport));
        printf('%J\n', { port, closed: true });
    },

    scan: () => {
        let result = scan();
        if (result?.changed) pushLan();
        printf('%J\n', result?.doc ?? { skipped: true });
    },

    // Every fifteen minutes, and a minute or two after a new lease: a device
    // just plugged in is the one somebody wants a terminal to. A first scan a
    // few minutes after boot, once the clients have their leases again.
    'scan-service': () => {
        sleep((120 + time() % 120) * 1000);
        while (true) {
            // A fresh read each round: the LAN's address and the adoption
            // can change under a loop that runs for weeks.
            cursor = uci.cursor();
            let last = readScan();
            let due = time() - (last.at ?? 0) >= SCAN_EVERY ||
                      (leaseMark() != (last.leases ?? '') && time() - (last.at ?? 0) >= SCAN_SOONEST);
            if (due) {
                let result = scan();
                if (result?.changed) pushLan();
            }
            sleep(60 * 1000);
        }
    },

    // First and last DHCP contact, kept across reboots and upgrades: a lease
    // file says only when a lease ends, and nothing says when a device first
    // appeared. dnsmasq reports every interface it serves, the setup AP and
    // the USB port among them, so only a LAN address counts.
    //
    // Also the one place an address changes hands, so the counting follows
    // it (COUNTING above): the address this MAC held before, and whoever
    // held this one, keep what they counted.
    seen: (mac, ip) => {
        mac = normalMac(mac) ?? exit(0);
        clientIp(ip) || exit(0);
        let seen = readSeen(), at = time();
        let counting = lanNet() && length(capture(['nft', 'list', 'set', 'inet', TABLE, 'usage_up']));
        if (counting && seen[mac]?.ip && seen[mac].ip != ip) foldAddress(seen[mac].ip, mac);
        for (let other in keys(seen)) {
            if (other == mac || seen[other].ip != ip) continue;
            if (counting) foldAddress(ip, other);
            delete seen[other].ip;
        }
        seen[mac] ??= { first: at };
        seen[mac].last = at;
        seen[mac].ip = ip;
        // Forget what has been gone SEEN_KEEP, its address's counters folded
        // into its own first, as for a client that moved.
        let reserved = {};
        cursor.foreach('dhcp', 'host', (s) => {
            let m = normalMac(type(s.mac) == 'array' ? s.mac[0] : s.mac);
            if (m && index(s['.name'], 'lan_') == 0) reserved[m] = true;
        });
        for (let other in keys(seen)) {
            if (other == mac || reserved[other] || at - (seen[other].last ?? 0) <= SEEN_KEEP) continue;
            if (counting && seen[other].ip) foldAddress(seen[other].ip, other);
            delete seen[other];
        }
        writeSeen(seen);
    },

    // From the init script's stop, and pulzr-firmware before a sysupgrade:
    // every client's totals, for the next boot's table to start from.
    'save-usage': () => {
        if (!lanNet() || !length(capture(['nft', 'list', 'set', 'inet', TABLE, 'usage_up']))) exit(0);
        let ipOf = {};
        for (let l in leases()) if (l.mac) ipOf[l.mac] = l.ip;
        let seen = readSeen();
        for (let mac in keys(seen)) if (seen[mac].ip) ipOf[mac] ??= seen[mac].ip;
        let now = online();
        for (let mac in keys(now)) ipOf[mac] ??= now[mac];
        fs.mkdir(STATE);
        writeJson(USAGE_SAVED, { epoch: usageEpoch(), boot: bootId(), at: time(), clients: usage(ipOf) });
        system(['sync']);
    },
};

let cmd = commands[ARGV[0] ?? 'status'];
cmd ?? die('usage: pulzr-lan {apply|status|policy|cellular|reserve|unreserve|forward|unforward|expose|unexpose|address|links|seen|isolate|scan|scan-service|save-usage}', 2);
call(cmd, null, null, ...slice(ARGV, 1));
